mixing colors is second nature for young  Sulaiman abdur-rahman but after painting  for years it was only when Sudan's  Revolution began in December that he  started feeling like a real artist and  that every stroke of the brush now gives  him the chance to show his talent as  well as feel like a part of the  revolution by spreading its messages  [Music]  people have become close to art since  the Revolution and they've started  paying more attention to it so this is  to show the Sudanese art to bring it out  Sudan's visual art has been oppressed  people from the field were not knowing  there was a clamp down on art for 30  years now we want to bring that out  since anti-government protests started  last December over the rising costs of  bread and fuel artwork has appeared on  walls in Sudan's capital many told then  President Omar al-bashir his time was up  and so it was his 30-year rule brought  to an end by the military after a  long-running sitting where every wall  was covered with colors that reflected  the demands of the protesters and their  hopes for Sudan's future art has become  a symbol of defiance for many Sudanese  here in Khartoum these walls were  painted by pro-democracy protesters in  April but whitewash by the military  following the killing of dozens of  protesters two months later within a few  weeks new paintings appeared and that's  not the only form of art that's thriving  poems and music was strictly regulated  during the shears rule with anyone  deemed to write anything viewed even as  remotely critical to the government  harassed or arrested now poets and  musicians say they feel no fear  poetry played a big role in the  revolution most of the slogans chanted  during protests came from poets who  wrote to fuel the revolution and  motivate the protesters it empowered  people and continues to the songs that I  used to sing at parties were turned down  by radio stations and there were other  artists who faced the same thing I'm  happy that such a day has come where we  can express our ideas through our music  not like the past where it had to be  careful about what I write in the song  the revolution was spearheaded by the  Sudanese professionals Association which  includes artists some of their work was  destroyed during the political turmoil  but they say the new work should be  preserved for future generations that  people will carry the messages of those  drawings and other arts for what we wish  is that these arts are now preserved for  future generations so that they know  about the history and the role these  arts have played in the Revolution and  the lives sacrificed for it to succeed  so dance revolution provided space for  Suleiman and many other artists to  unleash their creativity giving him  freedom which he says he'll guard with  every color he paints Heba Morgan al  Jazeera Khartoum
 

